      "writtenOn": "2025-02-26T05:29:44Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I have checked the source again and it seems nontrivial to keep *both* the current scaled_mult implementation *and* the SkASSERT in constrained_add, because the assertion is there exactly for guaranteeing the invariant that the current scaled_mult is breaking, and there\u0027s no way with the current LoongArch hardware to do exactly \"mulhrs\" with \u003c5 insns.\n\nIf we are to keep the current scaled_mult implementation on LoongArch, we\u0027d effectively lose the ability to test debug builds on LoongArch. Or we\u0027d have to disable the checks in constrained_add in case of LSX or LASX, and keep rendering slightly differently (thus leaking information that the user is using this niche platform -- pretty high entropy for canvas fingerprinting techniques).\n\nP.S. I just calculated exact costs for the old vs new implementation, based on uarch information on https://jia.je/unofficial-loongarch-intrinsics-guide.\n\nlatency before: xvmuh.h 4 + xvslli.h 1 \u003d 5 ticks (LA464 and LA664)\nlatency after:  xvmulw{ev,od}.w.h 4 + xvsrari.w 3 + xvpackev.h 1 \u003d 8 ticks (LA464 and LA664)\n\nAlthough the cost is going up 60%, depending on the use case this may or may not be a problem. I\u0027d strongly prefer correctness over performance unless concrete numbers prove otherwise (10%+ FPS drop for real-world use cases IMO).",
      "parentUuid": "d61d8a59_ef787c40",
